  per-object log10(sphere/FM12): min +0.0095, median +0.0666, max +0.2232 dex
  objects where FM12 predicts MORE acceleration than the exact sphere average: 0 of 14
      canonical: sphere +0.064 dex, FM12 +0.157 dex -- same sign: True
            alt: sphere +0.065 dex, FM12 +0.158 dex -- same sign: True
  [PASS] V5 the 'systematic' is one-sided with a known sign and a known cause: FM12 eq.60 under-predicts the exact sphere average at every object in the matched set, so it inflates every residual, and both defensible answers are POSITIVE on both footings.  The interval between them does not contain zero and does not change any conclusion.  A one-sided offset whose sign and size are computable is a correction that should be applied, not an uncertainty that should be carried   (all 14 per-object shifts have the same sign (sphere >= FM12); footings give sphere/FM12 = canonical +0.064/+0.157; alt +0.065/+0.158)

==========================================================================================================================
PART F.  V6.  f09's max() BRANCH IS NOT A CANDIDATE PRESCRIPTION AND CANNOT ENTER A SYSTEMATIC BUDGET.
==========================================================================================================================
  g05's own check P3 proves max() is discontinuous.  Here it is measured against the exact flux theorem,
  which any admissible QUMOND prediction must satisfy identically.
     |log10(max()/exact)| over the matched set: median 0.541 dex, max 1.246 dex
  [PASS] V6 f09's max() branch violates the exact QUMOND sphere-average identity by up to about half a dex on the very objects in question, and is discontinuous by g05's own P3.  Its -0.525 dex / -4.06 sigma is the signature of a wrong formula, not one arm of a prescription ambiguity, and listing it among the numbers that make the separation 'not a measurement' overstates the ambiguity   (median violation 0.541 dex, worst 1.246 dex; note g05's own headline systematic (0.093 dex) correctly EXCLUDES this branch, but the claim's number list presents it alongside the two defensible ones)

==========================================================================================================================
PART G.  V7.  THE WELL-POSED COMPARISON, AND WHETHER THE CLAIM IS LOAD-BEARING AT ALL.
==========================================================================================================================
  A systematic is compared with the STATISTICAL error, not with the central value of a null.
      canonical: separation +0.064 +- 0.069 (stat), prescription shift 0.093 dex -> systematic/statistical = 1.33; total error 0.116, significance with the shift folded in 0.56 sigma
            alt: separation +0.065 +- 0.069 (stat), prescription shift 0.093 dex -> systematic/statistical = 1.34; total error 0.116, significance with the shift folded in 0.56 sigma
  [PASS] V7 the well-posed statement is much milder than the claim: the prescription shift is comparable to the STATISTICAL error, about 1.3 times it, so folding it in as an error moves 0.93 sigma to about 0.6 sigma.  That is a sensitivity statement about a null.  It is not 'the signal is smaller than its systematic', and it is not what makes the number a non-measurement   (systematic/statistical = 1.33; significance 0.93 sigma bare, 0.56 sigma with the shift folded in as an error)
  [FAIL] V8 (THIS CHECK ASKS WHETHER THE SEPARATION WOULD BE A RESULT WITHOUT ANY SYSTEMATIC.  IT FAILS -- AND THAT FAILURE IS WHY S2b IS NOT LOAD-BEARING) the separation does not reach two sigma on either footing before a single word is said about prescriptions.  g05's conclusion that it is not a measurement therefore follows from 0.93 sigma and p ~ 0.10 alone.  Deleting S2b entirely changes nothing g05 concludes, so the systematic-versus-signal argument is reinforcement of an already-established null, not 'the single most decisive line in the file'   (bare significance 0.93 sigma on the canonical footing and 0.94 on the alt; both are already consistent with zero before any prescription question is asked)

==========================================================================================================================
PART H.  V9.  WHAT ARGUES FOR THE CLAIM, RECORDED AGAINST MY OWN CONCLUSION.
==========================================================================================================================
  Wolf et al. 2010's estimator M_1/2 = 3 sigma^2 r_1/2 / G is derived from the SPHERICAL Jeans equation.
  A uniform external field makes the effective gravity anisotropic (Milgrom 1986: enhanced by (1+L_e) along
  the field, unenhanced transverse), so the potential is NOT spherical and the estimator is not exact for
  either treatment.  A real prescription systematic therefore almost certainly exists.  Its size is bounded
  below by the anisotropy of the exact field over the matched set, computed here:
     pole-to-equator spread of the exact radial field: median 0.030 dex, max 0.218 dex
  [FAIL] V9 (THE STRONGEST CASE I CAN BUILD FOR THE CLAIM, PUT AS A CHECK: IS THE REAL EXTERNAL-FIELD MODELLING SYSTEMATIC AT LEAST AS BIG AS THE 0.093 DEX CLAIMED?  IT FAILS.)  A genuine systematic does exist -- the Wolf et al. 2010 estimator assumes a spherical potential and the external field breaks that -- but its natural scale is the anisotropy of the exact field, and across the matched objects that is a median of only about 0.03 dex, three times SMALLER than the 0.093 dex the claim carries.  So the claimed systematic is not merely mis-derived; it is larger than the physical effect it is standing in for.  The honest budget would be a few hundredths of a dex, well inside the 0.069 dex statistical error   (median pole-to-equator anisotropy 0.030 dex, worst 0.218, over the 14 matched objects, against the claimed prescription systematic of 0.093 dex and a statistical error of 0.069 dex.  Sizing the real effect properly needs an anisotropic Jeans solve, which neither treatment performs; this is a scale estimate, not that solve)

==========================================================================================================================
PART I.  MUTATION CONTROLS.
==========================================================================================================================
  [PASS] M1 MUTATION: with nu = 1 the same quadrature returns the internal Newtonian field exactly at every external field, so the angle-average machinery invents no coupling of its own   (ratios 1.000000000000, 1.000000000000, 1.000000000000)
  [PASS] M2 MUTATION, AND IT IS THE REFUTATION IN ONE LINE: switch off the kernel (nu = 1) and the two 'prescriptions' become IDENTICAL to machine precision on the real objects.  The entire 0.093 dex therefore comes from the curvature of nu -- that is, from the geometry factor (1 + L_e/3) against (1 + L_e) -- and not from any freedom in how the external field is treated.  A quantity that vanishes identically when the kernel is switched off is a property of the kernel, computable, not an uncertainty   (max |sphere/FM12 - 1| under nu = 1 over the 14 matched objects: 2.220e-16)
  M3 MUTATION on the DATA rather than the theory: multiply every observed dispersion by sqrt(2).  Every raw
  residual must move by exactly log10(2), and the sphere-minus-FM12 shift must not move at all.
  [PASS] M3 MUTATION on the DATA: inflating every observed dispersion by sqrt(2) moves every raw residual by exactly log10(2) and leaves the sphere-minus-FM12 shift bit-identical.  The 'systematic' therefore does not respond to the data at all -- it is a function of (x_i, x_e) and of nu alone.  Something that cannot change when the measurements change is not a measurement uncertainty   (worst departure of the residual shift from log10(2): 2.776e-16 dex; worst drift in the prescription difference across all 50 objects: 4.441e-16 dex)

==========================================================================================================================
VERDICT
==========================================================================================================================
  THE CLAIM IS REFUTED AS STATED, AND ITS CONCLUSION SURVIVES FOR A DIFFERENT REASON.

  REFUTED: 'the matched separation is smaller than its own external-field-prescription systematic'.
   * The two 'defensible treatments' are not two defensible treatments.  At 13 of the 14 matched objects the
     FM12 eq.60 value falls BELOW the exact QUMOND field at EVERY polar angle (V2), so it is not a value the
     exact solution takes anywhere -- an approximation biased low, not a rival prescription.  The bias is
     named: the eq.60 subtraction term x_e nu(x_e) = sqrt(x_e), applied to objects whose external field is
     three decades below their internal one and is doing nothing -- it accounts for 86-97 per cent of the gap
     at the near-isolated objects, and V2b is left FAILING at its 10 per cent bar rather than loosened
     because the last few per cent is second order in x_e.  The remaining structure is the
     analytic factor (1 + L_e/3)/(1 + L_e) to 1e-5 (V3), and the whole difference vanishes identically when
     the kernel is switched off (M2) and cannot respond to the data at all (M3).  The author's own stated
     weakest link -- 'arguably this is simply the approximation's error' -- is the measured position, not an
     arguable one, and 'I took the conservative branch' does not convert an approximation error into a
     systematic uncertainty on the exact calculation.
   * The shift is one-sided: FM12 under-predicts at all 14 objects, both answers are positive on both
     footings, and the interval between them never contains zero (V5).  A correction, not an error bar.
   * 'Systematic / signal = 1.4' is ill-posed because the denominator is a null.  Its bootstrap distribution
     is heavy-tailed with a 99th percentile of 29 and a maximum of 892 (V4), while the identical
     construction on the RAW offset -- a real signal -- is tight (V4b).  The number 1.4 is an accident of
     where a null central value happened to land, not a property of the data.
   * f09's max() branch, which supplies the -0.525 dex / -4.06 sigma in the claim's number list, violates the
     exact flux theorem by up to 1.25 dex and is discontinuous by g05's own P3 (V6).  It is a wrong formula,
     not one arm of an ambiguity.

  WHAT SURVIVES, AND IT IS g05's ACTUAL CONCLUSION RATHER THAN S2b's ARGUMENT FOR IT:
   * The matched separation is +0.064 +- 0.069 dex, 0.93 sigma, p ~ 0.10.  It is consistent with zero
     on its own, before any prescription question is asked (V8).  f09's 1.73 sigma remains withdrawn.
   * The well-posed systematic statement is systematic/statistical = 1.33, i.e. folding the prescription shift
     in as an error moves 0.93 sigma to 0.56 sigma (V7).  A sensitivity remark about a null.
   * A REAL external-field systematic does exist -- the Wolf et al. 2010 estimator assumes a spherical
     potential and the external field breaks it -- but it is SMALLER than the one claimed, not larger: the
     exact field's pole-to-equator anisotropy across these objects is a median 0.030 dex against the claimed
     0.093 (V9), and well inside the 0.069 dex statistical error.  Sizing it properly needs an anisotropic
     Jeans solve, which neither treatment performs.  This is the strongest case for the claim and it fails.

  AND ON THE ADVERSARIAL LENS -- does the claim DISCRIMINATE anything?  It cannot: it is a claim that a
  null is a null.  Ordinary cold dark matter makes no prediction about the size of a MOND external-field
  prescription systematic, so this line separates no hypothesis from any other.  g05's own S5 -- globular
  clusters below the kernel where dwarf spheroidals sit above it -- is the line in that file that does
  discriminate, and it argues for dark-matter content rather than for support type.  S2b is not the most
  decisive line in the file; S5 is, and A1b behind it.

